[
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">30</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">15</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋₊</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋₊+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"k\">show</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_num</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- this line normalizes the \"wrong way\" so that `floor_add_natCast` can apply</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor_add_natCast</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"o\">)]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I noticed that <code>Nat.floor_add_natCast</code> never applies if its argument <code>n</code> is a literal after a <code>norm_num</code>. In the example above, the <code>rw [... by norm_num]</code> is required to \"undo\" the previous <code>norm_num</code>.</p>\n<p>Is there a way to fix this? (My naive attempt: maybe a simproc that would match on the goal state after <code>norm_num</code>? But it should only apply to the positivity side condition)</p>",
        "id": 586240537,
        "sender_full_name": "Li Xuanji",
        "timestamp": 1776477437
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>would be nice to have a tactic or something that rewrites number literals into <code>Nat</code> with a <code>Nat.cast</code> in between</p>",
        "id": 586242246,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776478785
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Doesn't that just mean we should add a lemma <code>Nat.floor_add_ofNat</code>?</p>",
        "id": 586251683,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1776491509
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>are we doomed to add an <code>ofNat</code> and a <code>one</code> and a <code>zero</code> version for every lemma mentioning <code>Nat.cast</code></p>",
        "id": 586265393,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776507775
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>More or less, yes. There could be automation for it</p>",
        "id": 586271470,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1776513558
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought that we didn't need a separate <code>one</code> and <code>zero</code> version anymore, just an <code>ofNat</code> version?</p>",
        "id": 586308429,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776547412
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>no, since <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=instOfNatAtLeastTwo#doc\">docs#instOfNatAtLeastTwo</a> needs at least two so you don't get a diamond with one and zero</p>",
        "id": 586308479,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776547483
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why can't we have a type class assumption that includes one and zero? As in, one class with 3 instances, corresponding to the cases zero, one and at least two.</p>\n<p>I remember this issue being discussed about a year ago and I vaguely remember it being solved, but I might be wrong.</p>",
        "id": 586308688,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776547788
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>this still means you don't have an instance for <code>n</code> if you don't know whether <code>n</code> is zero or one or at least two</p>",
        "id": 586308749,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776547868
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But isn't that the whole point of writing an <code>ofNat</code> lemma? It's supposed to be used when there is a number literal.</p>",
        "id": 586308809,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776547943
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>but apparently you also want to write theorems about <code>ofNat(n)</code> for <code>n</code> a free variable</p>",
        "id": 586308883,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776548030
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right, so to prove such a theorem, you need an <code>OfNat n</code> instance, and a type <code>R</code> with a <code>Ring R</code> instance (or something weaker), and then another mystery <code>Prop</code> valued class asserting that <code>OfNat.ofNat n</code> is equal to <code>Nat.cast n</code>.</p>\n<p>Would this work?</p>",
        "id": 586309107,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776548383
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>perhaps</p>",
        "id": 586309181,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1776548502
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks all, indeed replacing that by <code>Nat.floor_add_ofNat</code> and <code>Nat.floor_add_one</code> (both of which are in Mathlib) allowed me to avoid the \"backwards rewriting\".</p>\n<p>While thinking about how to avoid this duplication of lemmas (at least from a user's point of view), I realized that these fit into the push lemma pattern.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">attribute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor_add_natCast</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">attribute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor_add_ofNat</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">attribute</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor_add_one</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⌊</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"bp\">⌋</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">push</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>(commenting out each <code>attribute...</code> line breaks one corresponding proof)</p>",
        "id": 588511527,
        "sender_full_name": "Li Xuanji",
        "timestamp": 1776734628
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Feel free to make a PR with <code>push</code> attributes for floors and ceilings <span aria-label=\"smiley\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f603\" role=\"img\" title=\"smiley\">:smiley:</span></p>",
        "id": 588562560,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776760397
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Opened at <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/38320\">#38320</a></p>",
        "id": 588614789,
        "sender_full_name": "Li Xuanji",
        "timestamp": 1776775704
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"479299\">@Jovan Gerbscheid</span> while you're here, do you think something like <code>det_mul</code> should be tagged with <code>@[push]</code>, if it's already tagged as <code>@[simp]</code>? It's already quite discoverable because <code>simp</code> will fire on it.</p>\n<p><strong> Pros </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>push det</code> is clearer than <code>simp only [det_mul]</code></li>\n<li><code>pull det</code> is clearer and more discoverable than <code>rw [← det_mul]</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong> Cons </strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>adds one more way to do the same thing (<code>simp only [det_mul]</code>, <code>rw [det_mul]</code>, <code>push det</code>)</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 588729121,
        "sender_full_name": "Li Xuanji",
        "timestamp": 1776808060
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think this is actually a common problem with <code>push</code> annotations. Most <code>push Not</code> and <code>push _ ∈ _</code> lemmas are also already <code>simp</code> lemmas. I'd say that <code>push</code>/<code>pull</code> lemmas are most useful when there are multiple, and they can be chained together. So if <code>det_mul</code> is the only <code>push</code> lemma for matrices, I think simply rewriting with that might be preferred over tagging it with <code>push</code>.</p>",
        "id": 588730526,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1776808766
    }
]